From 8c8143055948755589bc3be66d4d72560e02078f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(spec-decode): dead DSpark verification rows below a
 confidence cut

Adaptive verification picks how many drafts to verify on the host, before
the drafts it verifies exist: with async scheduling the budget comes from
the previous step's confidences, and the device can only redistribute it
between requests. At one stream that redistribution does nothing, and a
verified row costs several milliseconds of routed-expert reads.

With SPARK3_DSPARK_DEAD_ROWS_TAU=t the draft combine, which already writes
each request's draft rows, also computes each request's survival (running
product of this step's confidences, broadcast from rank 0) and marks the
rows past the first draft below t as padding. The routers give padding rows
no experts (VLLM_MOE_SKIP_PADDING) and B12X skips expert id -1, so dead rows
read no expert weights. Live rows are causal, so their outputs are
unchanged; the sampled-token fix-up commits at most one token past the last
live row (the rejection sampler also clamps before logprobs are taken). No
host sync and no batch-shape or graph change. Off by default; output is
unchanged because the target verifies every committed token.
